# 🗄️ Universal Ignore Patterns

# --- Language Specific ---

# Node & Tooling
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn*.log
.pnpm-store/
.eslintcache
# Yarn 2 and later keep their own state under .yarn/. Without zero-installs
# only these five folders are source, which is the list Yarn itself gives.
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/sdks
!.yarn/versions

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv
venv/
# Hypothesis example database (created by the local property-test run)
.hypothesis/
# The caches the common test, type and lint tools write beside the code.
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
# Anchored: a `downloads/` folder deeper down, such as assets/downloads/, is
# content, and an ignored folder takes the re-includes below with it.
/downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# ⚠️ `lib/`, `lib64/`, `bin/`, `parts/`, `var/` AND `ENV/` ARE DELIBERATELY
# ABSENT.
#
# They were here as virtualenv leftovers, and `.venv` / `venv/` above already
# cover every one of them. Unanchored, they also matched at ANY depth - and
# they are SOURCE directories in mainstream ecosystems, not build output:
#
#   lib/   every Ruby gem and Elixir application keeps its source there,
#          as do many Node packages; SvelteKit's $lib root is src/lib/
#   bin/   Rails ships bin/rails and bin/setup; npm packages ship bin/cli.js
#
# `git add` on an ignored path succeeds and does nothing, so the file simply
# was not there - no warning, no error. In a scaffold whose whole premise is
# that YOU choose the directory structure, quietly deciding a tree is
# disposable is the one thing it must not do. A project that really does put
# build output in `bin/` should anchor it: `/bin/`. `ENV/` is the same case on
# macOS and Windows, where git matches without regard to case by default: it
# swallowed every `env/` folder, Terraform's environments among them.

# Go & native binaries
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Rust
target/
**/*.rs.bk
cargo-home/

# .NET & Visual Studio
obj/
.vs/
*.user
*.suo

# Java / JVM
.gradle/
!src/main/**/build/
!src/test/**/build/
out/
.artifacts/
*.class
*.jar
*.war
*.ear
# ⚠️ THE WRAPPERS' OWN JARS ARE SOURCE. Every Gradle Wrapper and Maven
# Wrapper project commits them, and `./gradlew` and `./mvnw` cannot start
# without them - while `git add -A` would skip them without a word.
!**/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
!**/.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.rar
# An archive under assets/ is something the project presents - a font pack,
# a press kit - not build output, so it is kept.
!/assets/**/*.zip
!/assets/**/*.tar.gz
!/assets/**/*.rar

# C / C++
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.out
*.app
cmake-build-*/
.cache/

# Mobile (Android/iOS)
*.apk
*.aar
*.ipa
local.properties
.bundle/

# --- Environment & Secrets ---
# Always use .env.example for templates
#
# ⚠️ `.env.*` MATCHES EVERY VARIANT, and that is the point. Naming them one at
# a time missed .env.production, .env.staging, .env.development and .env.test
# - ordinary source files as far as git was concerned, while .env.example
# promised that every `.env*` was covered. A file holding real credentials
# must not depend on somebody having listed its exact name first.
#
# The negation keeps this repository's own example, and MUST stay after the
# pattern that would otherwise ignore it: git applies the last match.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# direnv's file usually exports the same secrets. A project whose .envrc
# holds none can delete this line.
.envrc
.direnv/
*.pem
*.key
# A Keynote deck in assets/ shares the extension and is not a key.
!/assets/**/*.key
# Keystores and private keys by their usual names. Public halves (`.pub`)
# are not matched and stay committable.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.ppk
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
# Credential stores by their usual names: package-registry and git logins,
# and Docker's, which holds registry passwords.
.pypirc
.netrc
_netrc
.git-credentials
**/.docker/config.json
# Terraform and OpenTofu state holds every secret a plan touched, in plain
# text, and a variables file is where the inputs to one are kept.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
crash.log
crash.*.log
firebase-debug.log
.firebaserc

# --- IDE & Editors ---
# The four VS Code files a team shares. `tasks.json` belongs with the other
# three - it is where build, test and run tasks are defined, so leaving it out
# meant `git add -A` silently dropped it and teammates never got the tasks.
# The README beside them logs the folder, and without a line of its own here
# it would be dropped the same silent way.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/README.md
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Build & Caches ---
#
# `dist/`, `build/`, `out/` and `.cache/` are NOT repeated here: the language
# sections above already carry them. Restating a pattern after a negation
# silently revokes it - git applies the last match, so the `build/` that used
# to sit here undid `!src/main/**/build/` fifty lines earlier and swallowed
# any Java package named `build`. A duplicate is never harmless in this file.
coverage/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
*.tsbuildinfo
# End-to-end runs: Playwright's report, traces and caches.
playwright-report/
test-results/
blob-report/
playwright/.cache/
.next/
.serverless/
.terraform/

# --- Docs Site (Jekyll / GitHub Pages) ---
# Local builds stay out of git. Nothing here publishes a docs site; wire that
# up yourself if you add one.
_site/
.jekyll-cache/
.jekyll-metadata
